---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import EnergyLandscape from '../components/EnergyLandscape.astro';
import { data } from '../lib/canonical';

const conflicts = Object.entries(data.conflicts);
---

<Base
  title="Colophon"
  description="Why there is an energy landscape on the front page, how it is computed, and what this site is built with."
  og="colophon"
>
  <div class="wrap col">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Colophon"
      title="About the figure"
      lede="Why there is a contour map on the front page, what it is computing, and what the rest of the site is made of."
    />

    <div class="col__grid">
      <div class="prose col__body">
        <h2 id="why">Why a landscape</h2>
        <p>
          An energy landscape is the picture I reach for most often, so it is the picture the
          site is built from. Several of the projects here do involve a barrier, a path, or a
          basin, and a few of them say so on their own pages. I am not going to claim more than
          that. A single frame covering all of the work would be tidier than the work actually
          is.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the figure earns its place with is narrower and checkable: it is a real function
          from the literature, every mark in it is computed rather than drawn, and the same
          numbers drive the small diagram at each section break. The rest of this page is what
          it is computing.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what">What the figure actually is</h2>
        <p>
          It is the Müller-Brown potential, a sum of four anisotropic Gaussians published in
          1979 as a test surface for path-finding methods. It has three minima and two saddle
          points, and it is the standard thing you draw when you want to show someone what a
          reaction path is. Choosing it rather than inventing a shape matters: it is a real
          function from the literature, and the picture is computed, not drawn.
        </p>
        <p>Everything in the figure is derived at build time from that function:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The contours</strong> come from marching squares over a sampled grid. Every
            fifth line is drawn heavier, the way an index contour works on a topographic map.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The stationary points</strong> are found by Newton refinement on the
            analytic gradient. The minima are labelled A, B, and C; the saddles carry the double
            dagger that transition states are marked with.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The dashed line</strong> is the minimum-energy path between the two deepest
            basins, computed with the simplified string method: evolve a chain of points down
            the gradient, redistribute them at equal arc length, repeat until it stops moving.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The orange trace</strong> is a particle under Langevin dynamics, integrated
            with a BAOAB scheme. In the static version it is dropped on the right-hand wall, at
            the open circle, and comes to rest in the global minimum, at the filled one. It
            darkens along its direction of travel, so the direction reads without arrows.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="live">What moves, and why it moves that way</h2>
        <p>
          When your browser allows motion, the same physics runs live, with one addition:
          well-tempered metadynamics. The walker deposits a Gaussian hill wherever it lingers,
          and each new hill is smaller where the accumulated bias is already large. The effect
          is visible: the basin the particle is sitting in slowly fills with orange contours
          until it is shallow enough to escape over a saddle, and then the process starts again
          in the next basin.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is not an animation chosen to look nice. It is the standard method for sampling
          a landscape whose barriers are too high to cross by waiting, and watching a basin fill
          is the most honest picture of what sampling a rough surface feels like.
        </p>
        <p>
          Moving the cursor over the figure adds a repulsive Gaussian at the pointer, which
          pushes the walker out of wherever it has settled. That is the leukemia project's
          framing made literal: a drug as a force that collapses a basin.
        </p>

        <h2 id="degradation">How it degrades</h2>
        <p>
          The whole figure is server-rendered SVG. With JavaScript off, with reduced motion set,
          on a slow connection, and in the first frame before anything loads, you get the
          complete static figure, including a trajectory. The canvas only ever overlays the
          orange layer, and it checks{' '}
          <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> before it starts, stops when scrolled out of
          view, stops when the tab is hidden, and can be paused by hand. If the script throws,
          the SVG underneath is still the figure.
        </p>

        <h2 id="motif">The recurring mark</h2>
        <p>
          The small diagram at each section break on the front page is the energy along that
          same minimum-energy path, as a reaction-coordinate profile. The marker advances one
          stop per section, from basin B at the top, over the first saddle, through C, and up
          to the second saddle at the last break. The global minimum A is the footer, which is
          why the last thing on every page is its energy. Reading the home page top to bottom
          is a traversal of the path in the hero figure, and the number next to the marker is
          the actual energy at that point. The other pages each get one still view of the same
          surface, numbered 0.1 to 0.4.
        </p>

        <h2 id="type">Type and colour</h2>
        <p>
          The typeface is Lato, which comes from the academic homepage template that a large
          fraction of researchers I read use, by way of Jon Barron and Tairan He. The two link
          colours are from the same stylesheet: <span class="swatch swatch--blue"></span>{' '}
          <code>#1772d0</code> for links and <span class="swatch swatch--orange"></span>{' '}
          <code>#f09228</code> on hover, with <span class="swatch swatch--hl"></span>{' '}
          <code>#ffffd0</code> as a highlighter.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I did not keep is the 14-pixel, table-based, single-column scale that template
          has carried since the mid-2000s. The type here is built on a major-third scale (ratio
          1.25) rebased so that body text is readable on a laptop, with the template's 14, 22,
          and 32 pixel sizes sitting on steps 0, 2, and 4 of it. Numbers are set with tabular
          figures everywhere they line up, which is most places on this site.
        </p>
        <p>
          The colour assignment follows the figure rather than the other way round: blue is the
          surface, and orange is what moves across it. The link hover is therefore the same
          event as the walker leaving a basin.
        </p>

        <h2 id="built">Built with</h2>
        <p>
          Astro, building to static files, deployed to GitHub Pages by a workflow on every push.
          Content is Markdown: adding a note means adding a <code>.md</code> file. Math is
          rendered at build time with KaTeX, so there is no client-side typesetting and no
          flash. Lato and Commit Mono are self-hosted as subsets with size-adjusted fallbacks,
          so no text moves when they load. There is no analytics and no tracking.
        </p>
        <p>
          The figures on the project pages are plain SVG generated from data files extracted
          from the project repositories, with no charting library. Each one names its source.
        </p>
        <p>
          The source for this site is <a href={data.site.repo} rel="noopener">on GitHub</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sources">Where the facts come from</h2>
        <p>
          Biographical facts, publications, honors and positions all read from one data file,
          so they cannot disagree between pages. Each project's numbers live with that project,
          and the figures are drawn from data files that name the repository they came from.
          My CV, resume, and activities list disagreed with each other in a few places, written
          as they were at different times for different readers. Where they did, this site takes
          the most conservative defensible version, and records the disagreement rather than
          hiding it, which is the table below.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="col__side">
        <EnergyLandscape mode="hero" />
        <p class="col__figcap">
          The same figure as the front page.<span
            class="col__figcap-motion"
          > Push it around while you read about it.</span>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <section class="col__conflicts" aria-labelledby="conflicts">
      <h2 id="conflicts">Source disagreements</h2>
      <p class="col__conflicts-lede">
        Each row is a place my own documents disagreed. The site uses the "shown" column.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Source disagreements">
        <table class="ctable">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">claim</th>
              <th scope="col">shown on this site</th>
              <th scope="col">why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              conflicts.map(([key, c]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{key.replace(/_/g, ' ')}</th>
                  <td>{c.chosen}</td>
                  <td class="ctable__why">{c.why.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim()}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .col {
    padding-bottom: var(--sp-6);
  }
  .col__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 22rem;
    gap: var(--sp-7);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .col__side {
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--sp-5);
  }
  .col__figcap {
    margin-top: var(--sp-3);
    font-size: var(--fs-small);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .col__figcap-motion {
      display: none;
    }
  }
  .col__body :global(code) {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.86em;
    background: var(--paper-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.08em 0.32em;
  }
  .swatch {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 0.78em;
    height: 0.78em;
    border-radius: 2px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-2);
    vertical-align: -0.03em;
  }
  .swatch--blue {
    background: var(--blue);
  }
  .swatch--orange {
    background: var(--orange);
  }
  .swatch--hl {
    background: var(--highlight);
  }

  .col__conflicts {
    margin-top: var(--sp-8);
    padding-top: var(--sp-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .col__conflicts-lede {
    margin-top: var(--sp-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-4);
    font-size: var(--fs-small);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .ctable {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 46rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-small);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .ctable th[scope='col'] {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--fs-tiny);
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink-2);
    padding: 0 var(--sp-4) var(--sp-2) 0;
  }
  .ctable th[scope='row'] {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: var(--fs-tiny);
    white-space: nowrap;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-4) var(--sp-3) 0;
  }
  .ctable td {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-4) var(--sp-3) 0;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .ctable tbody tr th[scope='row'] {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .ctable__why {
    color: var(--ink-3);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 64rem) {
    .col__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--sp-6);
    }
    .col__side {
      position: static;
      order: -1;
      max-width: 32rem;
    }
  }
</style>
